    "q-119289": {
      "dom_id": "step-q119289",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
